const e={slug:"voynich",title:"The Voynich Script",otherNames:["Beinecke MS 408 (Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library, Yale University)","Voynichese (an informal name for the script and its supposed language)","the “Roger Bacon cipher manuscript”, as Voynich and Newbold presented it (the attribution is legend)"],family:"Not a magical alphabet with a known key, but an unread script known from one manuscript, with no Unicode encoding and no accepted sound values. It stands beside the secret and cipher alphabets of the fifteenth to seventeenth centuries because it is the most famous unread script of the European tradition. No link with Trithemius, Agrippa or the angelic alphabets is documented.",keyedTo:"none",direction:"ltr",status:"ready",summary:"Beinecke MS 408 at Yale is a small illustrated book on vellum, written throughout in a script found in no other document. Radiocarbon tests place its vellum in the early fifteenth century; it is first documented in Prague, in 1639, and it takes its name from the bookseller Wilfrid Voynich, who bought it and made it famous. It has been read as a cipher, as a natural language in an invented script, as a constructed language and as a meaningless hoax; no reading has been accepted.",intro:["Beinecke MS 408 is a small book of vellum leaves, painted with plants, with diagrams of the stars and medallions of the zodiac, with figures bathing and with jars, and written from beginning to end in a script found in no other document. Its readers have named its sections from the pictures — herbal, astronomical, “balneological”, pharmaceutical — because the text has given them nothing else to name them by. No reading of it has been accepted.","Its history begins as a fact about the material and continues as a chain of letters. Radiocarbon tests place the skins of its vellum in the early fifteenth century; when the script was put on them is not known. In 1639 Georg Baresch of Prague, who owned the book, wrote to Athanasius Kircher in Rome for help in reading it, and in 1665 or 1666 Johannes Marcus Marci sent the book itself to Kircher, with a letter repeating what he had been told: that the Emperor Rudolf II had paid 600 ducats for it, and that its author was Roger Bacon. Both stories are hearsay, and Kircher, so far as is known, published nothing on the book. It came to light again in the hands of the bookseller Wilfrid Voynich, who bought it in 1912 and presented it as Bacon’s; it has been at Yale since 1969.","Then came the decipherers. William Romaine Newbold announced in 1921 that he had read it as Bacon’s Latin, and John Matthews Manly showed in 1931 that he had not. Professional cryptanalysts, William F. Friedman and John Tiltman among them, worked on it without result and began to count its glyphs and words; since the late 1990s it has been transcribed into EVA, a Latin-letter alphabet of its strokes, in which most counts are now made. The counts are the difficulty. The text has some of the statistics of a natural language and some that no European language has, and each kind of theory — a cipher, a natural language in an invented script, a constructed language, meaningless text — meets an objection in one set or the other.","It is not documented as a book of magic. It stands in this wing as the most famous unread script of the European tradition, in a book of astrological and herbal pictures, on vellum about a century older than the secret alphabets Trithemius and Agrippa printed — and, unlike theirs, it came with no key. Its history is given here as the standard accounts give it. The research behind the page could not yet check those accounts point by point, so the finer points that rest on them alone are left out; the first caution below says which."],firstAttestation:{work:"Beinecke MS 408 itself, the only witness of the script. The earliest document usually cited as mentioning it is a letter from Georg Baresch of Prague to Athanasius Kircher, 1639.",author:"Unknown. Roger Bacon, named in the seventeenth century, and Dee, Kelley and others, named in the twentieth, are legend or conjecture: see the claims.",date:"Early fifteenth century — The date of the vellum, not of the writing. Radiocarbon tests made at the University of Arizona and reported in 2011 are usually cited as giving 1404–1438. The first document to mention the book is of 1639.",year:1404,place:"Unknown. Central Europe and northern Italy have been proposed, from the costumes, from a castle with swallow-tail battlements on the page of “rosettes”, and from the Romance month names on the zodiac pages; none of these settles it. The book is first documented in Prague, in 1639.",locator:"Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library, Yale University, MS 408. Marci’s letter to Kircher is kept with it.",notes:"Radiocarbon dates the death of the animals whose skins became the vellum, not the writing. Most scholars take the writing to be close in date, because vellum was seldom kept blank for decades, but that is an inference. Before the tests, dates from the thirteenth century (for Bacon) to the sixteenth had been proposed. A faint inscription at the foot of the first leaf, read as the name of Jacobus Horcicky de Tepenec, a chemist at the court of Rudolf II, may be an older trace of an owner than Baresch’s letter; but it is written in the book, not in a separate document, and whose hand wrote it is debated."},drawing:{follows:"Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library, Yale University, MS 408, the only witness of the script, in the Library’s own digital images (Yale Digital Collections, object 2002046): sixteen glyphs from f. 2r (image 1006078), m from f. 4r (image 1006082), and p and f from f. 6r (image 1006086) — all in quire 1, the herbal section, in Currier’s language A and one hand. Each glyph is one instance in a named word, its folio and line as in the ZL transliteration of Zandbergen and Landini (version 3b, 13 May 2025). The images are not reproduced here: Yale’s record for them states no open licence.",drawn:"Each glyph is traced as the centre line of the scribe’s strokes, all at one scale from pages photographed at one scale, so that they keep their sizes relative to one another — an o a quarter to a third of a gallows’ height — but each is centred in its own box, not set on a common writing line. The drawing keeps what the ink shows: which loops close and which stay open, where strokes cross, the feet and flicks at the ends of minims, and a hairline where it runs on. The rest is the site’s convention: one even round pen for the scribe’s varying stroke, and for p and f an instance from mid-paragraph, where the first lines of paragraphs draw them larger and more freely. Of the four benched gallows only cth is drawn, and the class of rare glyphs, which has no one form, is not drawn.",variants:"There are no editions to differ, only one manuscript and its transcriptions, so each glyph was compared with itself elsewhere in the book: in other words of quire 1, in the same hand, and in the Currier B hands of f. 78r and f. 103r, which make the same constructions with differences of slant, size and length — y’s tail bends back in f. 78r, and sh’s plume is shorter and rounder there. Every traced instance was also compared with an older set of photographs of MS 408, uploaded to the Internet Archive in 2008 without a stated source, which show the same strokes. The transcriptions disagree on where words divide, on how many minims some runs hold and on the rare glyphs; and the hands differ — Prescott Currier saw at least two, and Lisa Fagin Davis has argued for five. Modern Voynich fonts and charts are not used as a base."},sample:{orders:[8,12,16,18],caption:"The four gallows, k, t, p and f, as written on ff. 2r and 6r."},lettersTitle:"The Glyphs",unitNoun:{one:"glyph",many:"glyphs"},scriptNote:"Twenty units, named in EVA, the transcription alphabet researchers commonly use. Each EVA letter was chosen for the look of a glyph and claims no sound: the script has no accepted values and no numbers. EVA writes some single glyphs as strings of strokes — the “bench” as ch, a gallows set over a bench as cth — so that a transcriber need not decide in advance which strokes make one sign. The example words are EVA spellings as researchers commonly quote them. Each drawn glyph is one instance, traced from a named word on a named folio. The order is the site’s own, for display; it is not a ranking by frequency.",letters:[{order:1,name:"o",value:null,translit:"o",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 52.8 42.3 C 57.1 41.9 59.1 46.1 58 50.4 C 56.8 55.1 52.5 58.2 48.9 57.9 C 44.9 57.6 43.2 53.3 44.1 49.7 C 45.3 45.4 49.1 42.7 52.8 42.3 Z"},strokes:"One small closed oval on the line, leaning slightly to the right, about as tall as the bowl of a.",note:"Like a Latin minuscule o: a small closed loop on the line. It often begins a word — ol, or, ok-, ot- — and q is almost always followed by it, as in qokeedy. Drawn from otchal, f. 2r, line 1."},{order:2,name:"e",value:null,translit:"e",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 49.5 41.7 C 44.8 40.3 39.1 41.9 37.7 45.7 C 34.8 50.4 37.4 54.6 43 54.6 C 48.1 54.2 51.4 52.7 54.3 51.5"},strokes:"One small c-curve open to the right: from its head it turns left, down round the bowl and runs out to the right along the line. No tick at the head.",note:"A small curve open to the right, like a Latin c, narrower than the bench and not joined to its neighbour. It stands single in chedy and shedy, doubled in qokeedy, and is sometimes tripled. EVA calls it e for its look; it does not stand for the sound e. Drawn from qokey, f. 2r, line 9; no instance checked has a tick at its head."},{order:3,name:"ch",value:null,translit:"ch",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 39.1 42.9 C 35.8 44.3 35.2 50 35.8 52.4 C 36.5 56.2 41 57.2 48.1 56.5 M 39.1 42.9 L 65.2 42.2 M 71.3 42.4 C 67.1 42.4 64 44.8 63.7 49.1 C 63.8 53.3 66.7 55.6 70.9 55.7 L 74.4 55"},strokes:"Two c-curves side by side on the line, joined across their heads by one straight stroke that runs from the head of the left c to the head of the right c, whose head projects a little beyond the join.",note:"The “bench”, as researchers call it: two c-curves side by side, joined across their tops by one short stroke. EVA spells it with two letters because it writes the script stroke by stroke, but on the page it behaves as one sign, at the start of words and within them: chedy, chol, chor, chey. EVA’s c and h scarcely occur except in this sign, in sh and in the benched gallows. Drawn from chy, f. 2r, line 3: the joining stroke runs straight from the head of the left c to the head of the right one, which projects a little beyond it."},{order:4,name:"y",value:null,translit:"y",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 54.6 32 C 48.1 30.6 43.4 34.8 43.6 39.6 C 43.9 45.3 48.6 47.6 52.4 45.4 M 53.8 31.6 C 57.4 33.7 60.4 39.4 60.6 46.5 C 60.9 54.7 58.1 63.3 53.2 69.5"},strokes:"A small bowl, and from its head a stroke that bulges out to the right and runs down well below the line, ending towards the lower left: a 9.",note:"A bowl like that of a, with the stroke on its right running down below the line, so that it looks like a numeral 9; the older transcriptions wrote it so. Common at the end of words (-dy, -ey: chedy, qokeedy), it also begins them. Drawn from chy, f. 2r, line 3: in this hand the tail runs down to the lower left and does not curl back; in the Currier B hand of f. 78r it is longer and bends back to the left at its foot."},{order:5,name:"a",value:null,translit:"a",numeric:null,formConfidence:"medium",glyph:{d:"M 53.8 43.4 C 49.1 41.5 45.3 44.8 45.3 49.5 C 45.6 53.5 50 56.2 55.8 54.6 M 53.4 43 C 55.2 46.7 56.6 50.9 57.6 54 C 58.8 55.2 60.9 54.1 62.6 52.3"},strokes:"A small bowl (a c-curve) closed against a short minim on its right; the minim slants from the head of the bowl down to the line and ends in a small flick up to the right. Nothing rises above or falls below the line.",formReason:"the bowl and the down-stroke merge in the ink",note:"A small round bowl with a short down-stroke against its right side, like a one-storey Latin a; nothing rises above the line. It is usually followed by i, l, r or n: aiin, daiin, al, ar, dar. Drawn from daiin, f. 2r, line 1. At this size the right side of the bowl and the down-stroke merge into one dark band, so whether the bowl is a c joined to a minim or a closed loop with a stem cannot be seen; the down-stroke ends in a small flick up to the right."},{order:6,name:"d",value:null,translit:"d",numeric:null,formConfidence:"medium",glyph:{d:"M 55.6 44.4 C 57.9 41.6 58.2 37 53.3 35.5 C 48.4 34.2 46.6 38.9 47.7 42.7 C 49.5 46.3 52.8 48.4 54.6 50.5 C 56.9 53.9 55.3 61 50.3 62.8 C 44.6 64.1 41.7 61 42.7 57.5 C 43.4 53.9 46.7 51.3 50.5 50.4"},strokes:"Two loops one above the other like an 8, in one stroke: from the right of the upper loop over its head and down its left side, diagonally across the waist to the right, round the lower bowl (down its right side, under it, up its left side) and back to the waist. The upper loop ends just short of the waist.",formReason:"the upper loop is open here and closed elsewhere",note:"Like a numeral 8: a lower bowl and, above it, a smaller loop made by the same stroke. The older transcriptions wrote it 8. It begins the much-repeated word daiin and often stands before y: dy, chedy. Drawn from daiin, f. 2r, line 1, where the upper loop stops a hair short of the waist and is just open; in other instances, such as dals on f. 2r, line 4, it closes."},{order:7,name:"i",value:null,translit:"i",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 51.6 44 C 53.3 48.4 55.1 52.9 56.9 56.2 C 58.2 57.5 60.1 56.9 62 55.2"},strokes:"A single short minim slanting down to the right and ending on the line in a small foot turned up to the right; no dot.",note:"A single short stroke, a minim, with no dot; in this hand it slants down to the right and ends on the line in a small foot turned up to the right. It comes in runs of one, two or three, rarely four, and every run ends in n, r, l or m — the ii of aiin and daiin. Transcriptions disagree on how many minims some runs hold, one reason EVA writes them stroke by stroke. Drawn from the first i of daiin, f. 2r, line 1."},{order:8,name:"k",value:null,translit:"k",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 37.5 27.3 C 36.7 39.7 37.1 58.6 36.7 73.8 M 37.1 43.1 L 50.2 43.6 M 52.6 42.1 C 59.5 41.1 64.7 33 64.2 27.3 C 63.8 23.1 57.6 22.1 54.3 22.6 C 51.4 23.1 50.2 27.3 49.9 33 L 49.6 60.5 C 49.9 66.2 49.8 71.4 50.4 73.6 C 51.1 75 53 74 54.4 71.1"},strokes:"A gallows: two upright stems about three times the height of o, joined by a short bar a little above their middle; the right stem rises into a closed loop that swells out to the right above the bar; the right stem ends on the line in a small foot turned to the right.",note:"A “gallows”: one of four tall glyphs with an upright stem and a loop at the top, and with t one of the two plain ones. Common inside words, mostly after o or e — qokeedy, qokedy, okaiin — and also after the bench, as in chky. It has two stems joined by a short bar and one loop, rising from its right stem; t has a second, smaller loop to the left. Drawn from kol, f. 2r, line 9."},{order:9,name:"l",value:null,translit:"l",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 43.4 65.6 C 44.6 61.8 47.6 56.8 49.9 52.8 C 52 48.1 54.8 41.9 56.3 36.9 C 51.5 37.2 45.9 39.3 43.3 43.2 C 42 46.7 43.4 50.5 46.8 53.2 C 51.2 55.8 55.8 58.4 59 60.8"},strokes:"A pointed loop over a crossing: from the lower left a stroke rises to the right to a point at the top right, turns back across the top and down the left side, crosses the first stroke at the waist just above the line, and runs out to the lower right.",note:"Often likened to a cursive Latin l. As drawn from chol, f. 2r, line 5, it is a pointed loop rising only a little above o, made by a stroke that crosses itself just above the line, with its two free ends pointing down to the left and right; the lower-left end thins to a hairline whose length varies from word to word. Common at the end of words and before o or k: ol, al, chol, shol, lkeedy."},{order:10,name:"r",value:null,translit:"r",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 52.7 37.7 C 52.7 33.9 56 32 59.5 32.5 C 63.7 32.9 65.2 37.7 64.6 41.9 C 64 47.6 60.3 51.6 56.8 54.6 C 53.4 55.2 50.8 57.2 51.2 60.2 C 51.8 63.4 57 65.4 62.6 67.6"},strokes:"A short stroke like a numeral 2: a small open hook at the top left, an arch over to the right, a slant down to the left into a small rounded base on the line, from which the foot runs out down to the right.",note:"A short glyph ending on the line, which early transcribers likened to a numeral 2: an open hook at the top, not a loop, an arch, and a small rounded base on the line from which the foot runs down to the right. Mostly at the end of words — or, ar, dar, chor — or before a final y, as in shory. Drawn from dorchory, f. 2r, line 2. It is easily confused with s, which ends in a full c-curve at the foot."},{order:11,name:"q",value:null,translit:"q",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 61.4 21.6 C 51.9 26.3 41.5 31.5 35.4 36.2 C 40.5 39.1 45.3 40.7 50 40.1 C 55.7 39.6 61.4 38.4 67.1 37.7 M 61.4 21.6 C 62.1 31 61.4 45.3 60.7 54.7 C 60.1 64.2 59 72.8 57.9 79.4"},strokes:"Like a numeral 4: a stem that rises above the x-height and runs well below the line; from its top a stroke slants down to the left to a sharp point and returns to the right as a level bar, crossing the stem at about x-height and running on towards the next glyph.",note:"Like a numeral 4; the older transcriptions wrote the pair qo as 4o. It is almost always the first glyph of a word and almost always followed by o: qokeedy, qokain, qotedy. Its head closes in a point, not a loop, and its stem runs well below the line. Drawn from qoy, f. 2r, line 8."},{order:12,name:"t",value:null,translit:"t",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 41.3 22.5 C 40.5 35.8 40.1 54.7 39.4 76.1 M 45.7 21.6 C 38.6 21.6 30.1 25.3 28.7 31 C 28.2 33.4 32 34.6 36.7 34.8 L 52.8 36.9 C 59.5 37.5 67.5 35.8 72.3 32.5 C 75.1 29.6 73.2 24.4 68.5 22.5 C 63.3 21.1 56.6 21.6 54.3 25.3 C 53 28.2 52.8 32 52.8 36.9 C 52.3 50 50.9 64.2 49.8 76.8 C 50.8 77.5 52.4 77 53.6 75.6"},strokes:"A gallows with two loops: two upright stems hang from one long stroke about two-thirds of their height up; at each end this stroke curls up into a loop, a small one to the left over the left stem and a broad one to the right over the right stem; the right stem ends on the line in a small foot.",note:"The second plain gallows: tall like k, but with two loops, a small one to the left and a broad one to the right, curled up from the ends of one long stroke from which its two stems hang. Common inside words — qotedy, otedy, chtey — and it is the gallows of the benched cth. Drawn from otchal, f. 2r, line 1."},{order:13,name:"sh",value:null,translit:"sh",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 39.1 62.1 C 35.3 62.7 33.9 67 34.6 69.8 C 35.6 73.1 39.6 73.4 44.3 73.1 L 51.4 72.5 M 39.1 62.1 C 45.3 59.9 51.9 60.6 58.1 60.5 L 79.2 58.6 M 74.4 60 C 70.6 62.2 67.2 65.4 66.9 68.8 C 66.8 72.4 69.4 74.5 73.4 74.6 L 83.4 73.4 M 58.3 60.3 C 60 52.8 60.6 45.2 59.5 40 C 58.5 35.2 57.1 30.5 55.5 30.2 C 53.3 30 50.5 35.7 50.8 41.9 C 50.9 43.1 51.7 44 52.4 44.6"},strokes:"The bench ch with a plume: from the joining stroke, between the two c-curves, a stroke rises high above the line, turns over at the top and comes down a short way on the left, as a tall narrow hook open at the bottom.",note:"The bench with a “plume”: a small curved stroke rising from its top and arching back over it. Common at the start of words: shedy, shol, shory. In shol, f. 2r, line 8, from which it is drawn, the plume springs from the joining stroke, not from the left curve; in the Currier B hand of f. 78r it is shorter and rounder."},{order:14,name:"n",value:null,translit:"n",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 54.4 54.8 C 55.2 58 57.6 62.2 60.6 64.3 C 63.8 66 67.5 65.6 70.9 62.7 C 75.1 58.4 76.1 51.8 73.2 47.1 C 69.4 40.4 61.4 36.6 51.9 36.2 C 43.4 33.8 33.9 33.4 27.2 35.3"},strokes:"A minim whose foot turns into a broad curve: from its head it runs down to the line, rounds to the right and up, swells out to the right and returns to the left as a long, nearly level stroke above the x-height, reaching back over the i-minims before it.",note:"Closes a run of i-minims: a minim whose foot turns to the right into a broad curve, which swells out and returns to the left as a long stroke arching back over the minims before it, so that iin looks a little like a cursive iiv. Almost only at the end of a word, after a or i: aiin, daiin, qokaiin, ain. Drawn from the end of daiin, f. 2r, line 1."},{order:15,name:"s",value:null,translit:"s",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 45.7 37 C 43.4 37.5 41.9 35.6 43.2 34.1 C 45.3 31.7 51.9 31.2 57.6 32.2 C 62.8 33.1 65 38.6 64.5 43.4 C 64 49.1 58.1 52.2 50.9 53.8 C 43.4 54.4 39.2 56.4 39 60.9 C 39 66.2 42.4 68.9 46.4 68.7 C 50.2 68.3 53.5 66.6 55.9 63.5"},strokes:"A c-curve on the line whose head runs on up to the right into a plume that arches over to the left and ends in a small hook above the c; the foot of the c turns up at the right.",note:"Standing alone it is described as the left half of sh — a c-curve with the same rising plume — which is why EVA chose the letter, so that sh is s with h. Found at the start and the end of words: sol, sar, chs. Some transcribers confuse it with r. Drawn from the word s, one glyph alone, on f. 2r, line 8: a c-curve whose head runs up into a plume arching over to the left, its foot turning up at the right."},{order:16,name:"p",value:null,translit:"p",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 43.6 20.6 C 43.6 35.8 44.3 54.7 44.2 69 C 44.2 75 43.9 79.6 43.5 83.4 M 49.2 21.8 C 44.5 20.2 38.5 19.4 33.5 20.3 C 28.4 21.4 25 25.3 24.9 29.6 C 25.1 33.6 29 35.9 33.3 36.5 L 62.2 37.5 C 61.7 31 63.6 23 71.2 20.6 C 76.9 19.2 83.1 22 83.1 26.8 C 83.1 32.5 75 36.2 67.4 37.3 L 62.2 37.5 C 64.6 42 66 48.5 63.3 52.4 C 57.5 54.2 48.4 53.1 42.8 52.8 C 36.1 52.5 29.5 52.2 25.7 54.4 C 20.9 57.9 17.6 64.7 15.7 70.4"},strokes:"An ornate gallows: one tall stem; at its top a loop, whose top begins just right of the stem, curls out to the left and returns as a bar to the right, where a broad loop rises; from the foot of that loop a short upright drops to a second bar, which runs back left across the stem and sweeps down to the lower left as a long leg.",note:"One of the two ornate gallows. Like f, it stands mostly in the first line of a paragraph, as in pchedy, where it is drawn larger and with a freer hand. Drawn from pchar, f. 6r, line 8, in mid-paragraph: it has the small left loop and broad right loop of t, and adds a second bar that crosses the stem and sweeps down to the lower left as a long leg. The ink of f. 6r is pale, but every stroke can be followed."},{order:17,name:"m",value:null,translit:"m",numeric:null,formConfidence:"medium",glyph:{d:"M 52.8 61.9 C 48.1 62.8 43.4 60 41.3 56.3 C 40.7 53.8 41.1 51.9 42.8 51.5 C 46.2 52.7 51.9 50.8 56.1 47.2 C 52.4 42.9 47.2 35.8 45.7 30.1 C 44.6 25.9 48.6 25.1 52.8 25.1 C 57 25.6 61.4 28.8 61.6 34.3 C 61.9 39.6 59.5 43.4 57.8 46.7 C 60.4 50.9 63.7 55.7 65.2 61.4 C 65.9 65.2 65.2 69 63.7 72.4"},strokes:"A small closed loop above the line, like the head of d; from its waist one stroke runs down to the left and curls back to the right as a small open hook on the line, and another runs down to the right, below the line, as a long tail.",formReason:"the lower-left hook is open here, a loop or blot elsewhere",note:"It closes a run of minims or follows a, almost only at the end of a word and often at the end of a line: am, dam, aim, oram. It is sometimes likened to n with a longer final stroke, but as drawn from chom, f. 4r, line 2, it is not like n: a small closed loop above the line, like the head of d, from whose waist a hook runs down to the left and a long tail down to the right, below the line. The hook is open here, and in other instances a filled loop or a blot of ink; a short tick just left of it may be the pen’s entry and is not drawn."},{order:18,name:"f",value:null,translit:"f",numeric:null,formConfidence:"medium",glyph:{d:"M 47.2 29.1 C 48.7 40.5 49.7 54.7 50.1 69 L 50.4 81.3 M 49.3 40.3 L 62.2 39.6 C 61.8 33.9 63.2 25.3 68.4 21.1 C 73.6 17.8 80.3 20.6 80.7 26.8 C 81.2 32.9 74.6 36.7 67.9 37.7 L 62.2 39.6 C 63 45.3 63.2 50.9 61.8 53.8 C 57.5 55.2 50.9 54 45.2 54.7 C 36.6 56.2 27.2 61.9 21.1 68.5"},strokes:"An ornate gallows like k: one stem; a bar from the stem to the right, where a broad loop rises; from the foot of the loop a short upright drops to a second bar that runs back left across the stem and sweeps down to the lower left as a long leg. No loop to the left of the stem.",formReason:"a faint mark beside the stem is not drawn",note:"The other ornate gallows. Like p, it stands mostly in the first lines of paragraphs. Drawn from foeear, f. 6r, line 3, in mid-paragraph: it has only the right loop of k, with the second bar and long leg of p. A faint, paler mark just left of the top of the stem, which has no clear shape, is not drawn."},{order:19,name:"cth, ckh, cph, cfh",value:null,translit:"cth, ckh, cph, cfh",numeric:null,formConfidence:"high",glyph:{d:"M 27.6 55.6 C 23.4 56.4 19.6 59.6 19.4 63.8 C 19.3 68.6 23.6 72.6 29 72.8 L 32.8 71.8 M 21.8 60.6 C 35.8 60.9 50 61.9 64.2 61.4 C 70.9 60.9 76.6 59.5 82.2 59.7 M 76.1 60.4 C 73.7 62.8 73.2 67.1 74.7 69.4 C 76.6 72.3 80.8 72.3 82.8 69.4 M 40.7 26.3 L 40.5 73.7 M 59 40.1 C 58.1 50 56.8 61.4 55.9 72.8 M 40.7 26.3 C 33.9 27.7 27.2 32.9 25.2 37.7 C 24.7 40 28.2 40.5 32 40.3 L 59 40.1 C 60 33.9 63.3 29.6 69 29.1 C 74.2 28.8 78.7 31 78.6 34.4 C 78.4 39.1 73.2 41.3 66.1 41.8 L 59 40.1"},strokes:"The gallows t standing in a wide bench: the left c of the bench stands outside t’s left stem and the right c outside its right stem; the bench’s joining stroke runs straight across both stems at about x-height; t’s two stems run from its loops at the top down to the line.",note:"A gallows set over a bench: the two c-curves of ch at its foot, and the bench’s joining stroke running into or through it. EVA spells it c, gallows, h, and each of the four gallows can be benched; cthy, ckhy and cphy are common words. Drawn: cth, from cthy, f. 2r, line 6, with the bench drawn wide enough for its two c-curves to stand outside the gallows’ stems; ckh, cph and cfh, built the same way, are not drawn separately."},{order:20,name:"g, x, v, b, u, j, z",value:null,translit:"g, x, v, b, u, j, z",numeric:null,glyph:null,formConfidence:"low",note:"Not one glyph but a class. EVA keeps these letters for rare glyphs, and writes many forms found only once or a few times as numbered codes, an ampersand followed by a number; researchers call them “weirdoes”. How many there are depends on the transcription, and no count is given here. No one drawing can stand for a class, so none is shown."}],transmission:[{slug:"vellum",title:"The vellum",kind:"manuscript",year:1404,end:1438,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,when:"early 15th century (the vellum)",description:"The animals whose skins became the vellum of MS 408 die, by radiocarbon, in the early fifteenth century. The script, the pictures and the text are put on the vellum at a date that is not known.",dating:"The range usually cited from the radiocarbon tests made at the University of Arizona and reported in 2011. It dates the skins, not the writing; the writing is usually taken to follow soon after, since vellum was seldom kept blank for long, but that is an inference."},{slug:"baresch-1639",title:"Baresch writes to Kircher",kind:"text",year:1639,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Georg Baresch of Prague, who owns the book, writes to Athanasius Kircher in Rome to ask for help in reading it. Kircher’s Coptic book, the Prodromus coptus of 1636, has made him the obvious man to ask. It is usually cited as the earliest document to mention the manuscript.",dating:"A faint name on the first leaf may record an earlier owner (see the claims); but it is written in the book, and whose hand wrote it is debated, so the letter of 1639 is the earliest dated mention usually cited, not necessarily the first trace of its owners."},{slug:"marci-letter",title:"Marci sends the book to Rome",kind:"text",year:1665,end:1666,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1665 or 1666",description:"Johannes Marcus Marci (1595–1667), physician and rector of the University of Prague, sends the book to Kircher with a covering letter. He repeats what a “Doctor Raphael” had told him: that the Emperor Rudolf II had owned it and paid 600 ducats to the man who brought it, and that Raphael took its author to be Roger Bacon. The letter travelled with the book and is kept with it at Yale.",dating:"The accounts give the year as 1665 or 1666, the last figure being read both ways. How the book passed from Baresch to Marci is not settled. Kircher, so far as is known, published nothing on it, and no reply of his to Marci about it is known."},{slug:"voynich-1912",title:"Wilfrid Voynich buys the manuscript",kind:"event",year:1912,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"The antiquarian bookseller Wilfrid M. Voynich (1865–1930) acquires the manuscript, with other books, from the Jesuits, and for years keeps its source secret. He presents it as a work of Roger Bacon, and it has carried his name ever since. He reports finding Marci’s letter with it.",dating:"The place usually given is the Villa Mondragone, near Frascati. How and when the book came there from Kircher’s Rome is the least certain stage of its history."},{slug:"newbold",title:"Newbold’s Roger Bacon",kind:"text",year:1921,end:1928,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1921; 1928",description:"William Romaine Newbold (1865–1926) announces the first widely known claim to have read the manuscript. He finds microscopic “shorthand” strokes within the glyphs and reads them, by anagram, as a Latin text of Roger Bacon’s. The reading is published after his death as The Cipher of Roger Bacon (1928)."},{slug:"manly-1931",title:"Manly’s answer",kind:"text",year:1931,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"John Matthews Manly shows in Speculum that Newbold’s microscopic strokes are cracks in the ink, and that his anagram method, having no fixed rules, could yield whatever text its user wanted. No one has defended Newbold’s reading since."},{slug:"cryptologists",title:"The cryptologists’ studies",kind:"text",year:1967,end:1978,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"After the study groups led by William F. Friedman, the work continues among cryptologists. John Tiltman describes the regular structure of its words in the NSA Technical Journal (1967); Prescott Currier finds that the herbal pages fall into two statistically distinct “languages”, A and B, which go with different hands; and Mary D’Imperio’s The Voynich Manuscript: An Elegant Enigma (1978), published by the US National Security Agency, surveys the research to its date and is still the standard account of it."},{slug:"yale-1969",title:"To Yale",kind:"event",year:1969,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"After Voynich’s death in 1930 the manuscript passes to his widow, Ethel Voynich, and his secretary, Anne Nill. The New York dealer H. P. Kraus buys it, cannot sell it, and gives it to Yale’s Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library, where it is MS 408."},{slug:"eva",title:"EVA: the script transcribed",kind:"event",year:1998,end:null,circa:!0,circaEnd:!1,when:"late 1990s",description:"Gabriel Landini and René Zandbergen devise EVA, a Latin-letter transcription that records the script stroke by stroke and can be pronounced, without deciding in advance which strokes make one glyph. Complete machine-readable transcriptions follow, and they are the basis of most later statistics."},{slug:"rugg-2004",title:"A table and a grille",kind:"text",year:2004,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Gordon Rugg shows that a table of word parts and a grille — a card with holes, of the kind Girolamo Cardano published in 1550 — can produce text with many Voynich-like features. He offers it as a possible method of hoax, not as proof."},{slug:"radiocarbon",title:"The vellum dated",kind:"discovery",year:2011,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Radiocarbon tests made at the University of Arizona place the vellum in the early fifteenth century. That rules out Roger Bacon, who died about 1292, unless the text is a later copy of a work of his, for which there is no evidence; and it means that a forger in the time of Dee, Kelley or Voynich would have needed a stock of blank vellum a century or more old."},{slug:"recent-studies",title:"Statistics and hands",kind:"text",year:2013,end:2020,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Marcelo Montemurro and Damián Zanette find words clustering by section, as keywords do in real texts (2013); Torsten Timm and Andreas Schinner propose that the text was generated by copying and altering earlier words, “self-citation” (2020); and Lisa Fagin Davis distinguishes five scribal hands (2020). These are the main recent lines of scholarship; none of them reads the text."},{slug:"facsimile-2016",title:"The Yale facsimile",kind:"text",year:2016,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"The Beinecke Library and Yale University Press publish the authorised full-colour facsimile, edited by Raymond Clemens, with essays on the book’s history, its making and the attempts to read it."}],claims:[{claim:"The vellum of MS 408 was made in the early fifteenth century.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Radiocarbon tests made at the University of Arizona and reported in 2011; the range usually cited is 1404–1438. They date the death of the animals whose skins became the vellum, not the writing, which is usually taken to be close in date.",sources:["University of Arizona, news release (2011)","Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (2016)"]},{claim:"Georg Baresch of Prague owned the manuscript by 1639 and asked Kircher to help him read it.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Baresch’s own letter to Kircher of 1639, usually cited as the earliest document to mention the book. Kircher’s work on Coptic is why Baresch turned to him.",sources:["Baresch’s letter to Kircher (1639)","Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (2016)","Zandbergen, The Voynich MS (website)"]},{claim:"Marci sent the manuscript to Kircher in 1665 or 1666.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Marci’s covering letter, which travelled with the book and is kept with it at the Beinecke. The year is read both as 1665 and as 1666, so both are given.",sources:["Marci’s letter to Kircher, with Beinecke MS 408","D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)","Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (2016)"]},{claim:"The Emperor Rudolf II owned the manuscript and paid 600 ducats for it.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Only Marci’s letter says so, and Marci gives it as what “Doctor Raphael” had told him: hearsay, written down more than fifty years after Rudolf’s death in 1612. No other document is known to confirm it. The letter gives Raphael no surname; his identification with Raphael Mnishovsky is a later inference.",sources:["Marci’s letter to Kircher (1665 or 1666)","Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (2016)"]},{claim:"Roger Bacon wrote the manuscript.",standing:"legend",evidence:"The opinion of Marci’s informant, as Marci reports it, taken up by Voynich and by Newbold. Bacon died about 1292, more than a century before the vellum was made, so he could be the author only if the text were a later copy of a work of his, which nothing supports.",sources:["Marci’s letter to Kircher (1665 or 1666)","Newbold, The Cipher of Roger Bacon (1928)","Manly, Speculum (1931)","University of Arizona, news release (2011)"]},{claim:"John Dee brought the manuscript to Prague and sold it to Rudolf II.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"A conjecture of Voynich’s, repeated by many since. No document puts the manuscript in Dee’s hands or records a sale of it to Rudolf; the conjecture leans on the hearsay of Marci’s letter.",sources:["D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)"]},{claim:"Edward Kelley, John Dee or Wilfrid Voynich forged the manuscript.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Proposed at various times as a hoax hypothesis, and not refuted outright. But since the radiocarbon tests any such forger would have needed blank vellum a century or more old, and Baresch’s letter of 1639 rules out Voynich.",sources:["Rugg (2004), with Kelley as one possible hoaxer","University of Arizona, news release (2011)"]},{claim:"The inscription at the foot of the first leaf is the signature of Jacobus Horcicky de Tepenec.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"A faint name, now legible only under ultraviolet light, has been read as that of Horcicky, chemist and pharmacist at the court of Rudolf II. Whether it is in his own hand or is an owner’s note added by someone else is debated. If it is his, it puts the book among Rudolf’s courtiers, though not in Rudolf’s own hands.",sources:["Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (2016)"]},{claim:"Newbold deciphered the manuscript as a work of Roger Bacon.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Claimed in 1921 and published in 1928; refuted by Manly in 1931, who showed that the microscopic “shorthand” was cracking of the ink and that the anagram method had no fixed rules. No one has defended the reading since.",sources:["Newbold, The Cipher of Roger Bacon (1928)","Manly, Speculum (1931)"]},{claim:"The text falls into two statistically distinct varieties, Currier A and Currier B.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Prescott Currier’s analyses, reported by D’Imperio: sections in different hands differ in their word and glyph frequencies. Later statistical work agrees, and the finding is widely accepted.",sources:["D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)"]},{claim:"Five scribes wrote the manuscript.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Lisa Fagin Davis’s palaeographical study (2020), building on Currier’s two hands. It is a scholarly finding that others are still testing, not a consensus.",sources:["Fagin Davis, Manuscript Studies (2020)"]},{claim:"The text has some statistical features of natural language.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Word frequencies roughly follow Zipf’s law, and some words cluster in particular sections as topic words do in real texts (Montemurro and Zanette). These are measurements. Whether they show that the text means anything is disputed, since a careful generating method could produce some of them.",sources:["Montemurro and Zanette, PLoS ONE (2013)","D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)"]},{claim:"The text also has features unusual for European languages.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The entropy of its glyph pairs is low (Bennett); its words are built from rigid slots, which Jorge Stolfi described as a paradigm; one- and two-glyph words are scarce and long words absent; and the same word, or nearly the same, often repeats two or three times in a row. These measurements are the main obstacle to every proposed reading.",sources:["Bennett (1976)","D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)","Stolfi, papers published online"]},{claim:"Hypothesis: a cipher, hiding a known language.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Held by Newbold, by Leonell Strong (1945), by Robert Brumbaugh (1970s) and by others, each with a different system. Simple substitution would keep the letter statistics of the hidden language, and the Voynich statistics match none; few ciphers known before about 1440 could give its word structure; and no proposed system has produced connected text that anyone else could reproduce.",sources:["D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)","Manly, Speculum (1931)"]},{claim:"Hypothesis: a natural language written in an invented script.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Many languages have been proposed: an East Asian or tonal language, on statistical grounds (Stolfi among others); Nahuatl, from the plants (Tucker and Talbert, 2013), though the vellum was made before Columbus sailed; Hebrew, by computational methods (Hauer and Kondrak, 2016 and after); and a proto-Romance language (Cheshire, 2019), rejected by medievalists. None has yielded connected text that can be checked.",sources:["Tucker and Talbert, HerbalGram (2013)","Hauer and Kondrak (2016)","Cheshire, Romance Studies (2019)"]},{claim:"Hypothesis: a constructed language.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Held by William F. Friedman: an a priori “philosophical” language whose words are built by classification, which would explain their rigid structure. John Tiltman, who reports Friedman’s view, did not share it: his own analysis suggested to him a mixture of different kinds of substitution. The objection is that the known schemes of that kind are of the seventeenth century, such as John Wilkins’s of 1668, and nothing like them is known from the early fifteenth. Without a key it cannot be tested.",sources:["D’Imperio, An Elegant Enigma (1978)","Tiltman, NSA Technical Journal (1967)"]},{claim:"Hypothesis: meaningless text, a hoax or the product of a mechanical method.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Rugg (2004) showed that a table-and-grille method could make text like this; Andreas Schinner (2007) argued that the statistics point to a stochastic process; Timm and Schinner (2020) proposed that the scribes made new words by copying and altering earlier ones. Against it stand the fifteenth-century date, the great labour involved, the clustering of words by section and the difference between Currier A and B.",sources:["Rugg (2004)","Schinner, Cryptologia (2007)","Timm and Schinner, Cryptologia (2020)","Montemurro and Zanette, PLoS ONE (2013)"]},{claim:"The zodiac pages carry month names in a Romance language, written in the Latin alphabet.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The month names are there, in a hand different from the main script and possibly later. Their language is disputed — Occitan, Catalan and French have each been proposed — and so is whether whoever added them could read the main text.",sources:["Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (2016)","Zandbergen, The Voynich MS (website)"]}],uses:[{use:"Writing the whole text of the book",evidence:"The sections are named from the pictures, not from any reading of the text: herbal; astronomical and astrological; “balneological”, the pages of bathing figures; cosmological; pharmaceutical; and a closing run of short paragraphs marked with stars, the “recipes”. Whether the book was ever used as a working herbal or medical handbook is unknown."},{use:"Labels on the pictures",evidence:"Single words stand beside plants, jars, stars and figures, especially in the pharmaceutical and astronomical sections. Because a label ought to name its picture, several proposed readings have started from them; none has been confirmed."},{use:"A prized curiosity in seventeenth-century Prague and Rome",evidence:"Baresch’s letter of 1639 and Marci’s of 1665 or 1666 show learned owners trying to have the book read, and turning to Kircher, known for his work on Coptic and on hieroglyphs. The story of the 600 ducats, though hearsay, shows how highly the book was valued, or was said to be."},{use:"A test case for cryptanalysis, and then for computational linguistics",evidence:"The study groups led by William F. Friedman, the studies of Tiltman, Currier and D’Imperio (1967–78), and the statistical and machine-learning work since the late 1990s, nearly all of it counting the text in EVA transcriptions."},{use:"EVA as a working alphabet",evidence:"Researchers write, search and count the text in EVA, quoting words such as daiin or qokeedy. EVA was designed so that transcribers need not settle in advance which strokes make one glyph. It is a tool for transcription, not a reading."}],quotations:[],links:[{to:"latin",relation:"The additions in the Latin alphabet — the month names beside the zodiac medallions, which are in a Romance language, and a few lines on the last page — and Marci’s Latin letter. Proposals that the script itself is abbreviated Latin, from Feely (1943) on, have not been accepted."},{to:"hebrew",relation:"One of the natural-language proposals (Hauer and Kondrak, 2016 and after) matched the text to Hebrew by computer. A statistical match of that kind is not a reading."},{to:"coptic",relation:"Kircher’s Coptic book, the Prodromus coptus (1636), is why Baresch, and later Marci, turned to him in Rome to have the manuscript read."},{to:"trithemius",relation:"Context, not kinship: Trithemius’s Steganographia, begun in manuscript in 1499, and his Polygraphia, printed in July 1518, show what learned secret writing looked like about a century after the Voynich vellum was made. No connection with the Voynich script is documented."},{to:"enochian",relation:"Voynich’s conjecture that Dee sold the book to Rudolf II, and the suggestion that Kelley forged it, are hypotheses without documents. The Enochian alphabet comes from the same circle with a documented record: Dee’s record, under 6 May 1583, of what Kelley reported seeing (British Library, Sloane MS 3188)."},{to:"theban",relation:"A contrast: a secret alphabet with a known key and a printed source, beside a script with neither."},{to:"signs-of-the-art",relation:"The zodiac pages show each sign as a picture — the ram, the bull — at the centre of a medallion. Whether they also use the astrological sign-glyphs gathered on that page is uncertain."}],cautions:["The history on this page is given as the standard accounts of the manuscript give it — D’Imperio’s survey of 1978 and the Yale facsimile of 2016 chief among them — and the research behind the page could not yet check it against them. The core dates are given; the finer points that rest on those accounts alone — the day of each letter, the price Kraus paid, the list of lost leaves, the wording of Marci’s letter, the frequencies of the glyphs — are left out until they can be confirmed.","No reading of any Voynich word is established. EVA words such as daiin and qokeedy are transcription labels, not pronunciations, and no “translation” of any of them is given here.","Radiocarbon dates the vellum, not the writing. The vellum is of the early fifteenth century; when the script was put on it is not known, though it is usually taken to be not long after.","Rudolf II, the 600 ducats and Roger Bacon all come from one hearsay report: Marci’s letter repeats what “Doctor Raphael” had told him. No other document is known to confirm any of the three.","That Dee owned the book is a conjecture, and that Kelley, Dee or Voynich forged it is a hoax hypothesis; no document stands behind either. Baresch’s letter of 1639 rules out Voynich.","The manuscript is not documented as having been used for magic. It stands in this wing as the most famous unread script of the European tradition, in a book of astrological and herbal pictures.","Each glyph is drawn from one instance in the manuscript, with its word, folio and line named; other instances and other hands differ in slant, size and detail. No modern Voynich font is used in its place.","The transcriptions disagree on where words divide, on how many minims a run holds and on the rare glyphs, so every count of words or glyphs depends on the transcription used. The glyphs on this page stand in a display order, not ranked by frequency."],sources:[{citation:"Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library, Yale University, MS 408 (the Voynich Manuscript), with the letter of Johannes Marcus Marci to Athanasius Kircher kept with it. The Beinecke publishes digital images of the whole manuscript."},{citation:"Yale Digital Collections, Beinecke MS 408 (“Cipher manuscript”), object 2002046: the Library’s digital images, from which the glyphs are traced (ff. 2r, 4r and 6r).",url:"https://collections.library.yale.edu/catalog/2002046"},{citation:"Raymond Clemens (ed.), The Voynich Manuscript (New Haven: Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library and Yale University Press, 2016): the authorised facsimile, with essays on the book’s history, its making and the attempts to read it."},{citation:"Mary E. D’Imperio, The Voynich Manuscript: An Elegant Enigma (Fort George G. Meade, MD: National Security Agency/Central Security Service, 1978): the standard survey of research to its date."},{citation:"Prescott H. Currier, papers on the manuscript’s two “languages”, A and B, and its hands, as reported in D’Imperio (1978)."},{citation:"John H. Tiltman, “The Voynich Manuscript: ‘The Most Mysterious Manuscript in the World’”, NSA Technical Journal (1967)."},{citation:"William Romaine Newbold, The Cipher of Roger Bacon, ed. Roland Grubb Kent (Philadelphia, 1928)."},{citation:"John Matthews Manly, “Roger Bacon and the Voynich MS”, Speculum (1931)."},{citation:"William Ralph Bennett Jr., Scientific and Engineering Problem-Solving with the Computer (1976): the entropy analysis of the Voynich text."},{citation:"Gordon Rugg, “An Elegant Hoax? A Possible Solution to the Voynich Manuscript”, Cryptologia (2004); and “The Mystery of the Voynich Manuscript”, Scientific American (2004)."},{citation:"Andreas Schinner, “The Voynich Manuscript: Evidence of the Hoax Hypothesis”, Cryptologia (2007)."},{citation:"Torsten Timm and Andreas Schinner, “A Possible Generating Algorithm of the Voynich Manuscript”, Cryptologia (2020)."},{citation:"Marcelo A. Montemurro and Damián H. Zanette, “Keywords and Co-Occurrence Patterns in the Voynich Manuscript: An Information-Theoretic Analysis”, PLoS ONE (2013)."},{citation:"Lisa Fagin Davis, “How Many Glyphs and How Many Scribes? Digital Paleography and the Voynich Manuscript”, Manuscript Studies (2020)."},{citation:"University of Arizona, news release on the radiocarbon dating of the manuscript’s vellum (2011)."},{citation:"Arthur O. Tucker and Rexford H. Talbert, “A Preliminary Analysis of the Botany, Zoology, and Mineralogy of the Voynich Manuscript”, HerbalGram (2013)."},{citation:"Bradley Hauer and Grzegorz Kondrak, “Decoding Anagrammed Texts Written in an Unknown Language and Script”, Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics (2016)."},{citation:"Gerard Cheshire, “The Language and Writing System of MS408 (Voynich) Explained”, Romance Studies (2019); cited as a rejected proposal."},{citation:"Jorge Stolfi, papers on the structure of Voynich words, published online."},{citation:"Gabriel Landini and René Zandbergen, EVA, the transcription alphabet of the script, with the machine-readable transcriptions built on it."},{citation:"René Zandbergen, The Voynich MS (website): the manuscript’s provenance, EVA, the transcriptions and the history of research."},{citation:"René Zandbergen and Gabriel Landini, the ZL transliteration of MS 408 (IVTFF file ZL3b-n.txt, version 3b of 13 May 2025), from the same website: the word and line readings by which each drawn glyph’s instance is named."}]};export{e as default};
